WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
A Cepheid repeatedly brightens and fades. Measure the

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.260
time between peaks to find its pulsation period.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.400
The period tells you its luminosity. Now compare

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.900
that known luminosity with the apparent brightness to

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.040
find distance. Follow the four steps: identify the

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.060
Cepheid, measure brightness and period, find luminosity, then

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.200
find distance. This is the standard candle method.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.420
The light curve and star are a schematic

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.780
illustration of the repeating measurement.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.290
The Great Debate asked whether spiral nebulae were

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:33.850
inside the Milky Way or separate galaxies. Shapley

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.630
placed them inside; Curtis argued that they were

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.670
galaxies themselves. The debate did not settle the

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.890
evidence. Hubble identified a Cepheid in Andromeda in

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.270
nineteen twenty-three. Its faint appearance and known luminosity

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.210
revealed a very large distance. Andromeda is about

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.490
two and a half million light-years away. The

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.710
distance measurement established that spiral nebulae are galaxies

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.930
beyond our own.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.240
More distant galaxies recede faster. Hubble's law connects

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.780
the quantities: velocity equals the Hubble constant times

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.720
distance. Use seventy-two kilometers per second per megaparsec

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.520
for the Hubble constant. A galaxy receding at

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.480
seventy-two thousand kilometers per second is one thousand

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.760
megaparsecs away: divide seventy-two thousand by seventy-two. Redshift

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.480
measures the fractional increase in wavelength. For the

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.160
small-redshift calculation, divide velocity by the speed of

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.140
light. Seventy-two thousand divided by three hundred thousand

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.620
gives zero point two four.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.890
The distances grow because space itself expands. Watch

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.030
the separations increase between the galaxies. A farther

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.770
galaxy gains more distance during the same interval.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.230
Now choose a different galaxy as the observer.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.890
The surrounding galaxies still move away, and the

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.930
farther ones still separate faster. Every galaxy sees

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.950
the same general pattern. There is no special

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.130
center of expansion. The expanding surface illustrates growing

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.850
separation, not galaxies flying away from a unique

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:55.650
central point.
